1
00:00:09,770 --> 00:00:14,420
Let's jump into talk
about congestion control.

2
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:17,020
In this video we're
going to talk about

3
00:00:17,020 --> 00:00:19,695
congestion control
in the general.

4
00:00:19,695 --> 00:00:22,880
Then the next video
we'll get more specific

5
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:26,920
about TCPs mechanism
for congestion control.

6
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:33,100
As a reminder, congestion
in the network will occur.

7
00:00:33,100 --> 00:00:36,860
This is what can lead to
packets being dropped.

8
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:39,260
Now the question is what if

9
00:00:39,260 --> 00:00:43,580
the senders could detect
this and back off as needed?

10
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:46,780
This is the general concept
of congestion control.

11
00:00:46,780 --> 00:00:49,880
Congestion happens in the
network and we're controlling

12
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:54,320
it by effectively sending
less into the network.

13
00:00:54,760 --> 00:01:00,280
Let's look at how
congestion happens.

14
00:01:00,280 --> 00:01:04,060
We've said this before,
but to remind you,

15
00:01:04,060 --> 00:01:07,460
whenever the send rate out of

16
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:12,220
a link is lower than
the receive rate.

17
00:01:12,220 --> 00:01:17,000
This could occur maybe
there's different bandwidths.

18
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:19,600
You have one gigabit per

19
00:01:19,600 --> 00:01:23,935
second port and a 10
gigabit per second port.

20
00:01:23,935 --> 00:01:27,040
If you're receiving off
of the 10 gigabit per

21
00:01:27,040 --> 00:01:29,220
second port and
trying to send out

22
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:31,700
the one gigabit per second,

23
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:33,700
well then there's a potential

24
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:36,440
for some congestion
to happen and you not

25
00:01:36,440 --> 00:01:38,400
to be able to send at

26
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:42,000
the full rate that
you're receiving.

27
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:44,780
Another might be if you have,

28
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:47,180
as we discussed with a router,

29
00:01:47,180 --> 00:01:50,220
these multiple ports,
but they're all

30
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:53,980
directing towards the
same output port.

31
00:01:53,980 --> 00:01:57,580
Here's a real simple
illustration.

32
00:01:57,580 --> 00:01:59,835
Let's say, the network,

33
00:01:59,835 --> 00:02:02,940
this router on the right hand,

34
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:11,630
can transmit out this link
one packet per unit time.

35
00:02:11,630 --> 00:02:14,080
It can receive on each of

36
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:17,080
its ports one packet
per unit time.

37
00:02:17,080 --> 00:02:20,760
The first time unit,

38
00:02:20,760 --> 00:02:25,020
we'll see that it
receives packets 1 and 2.

39
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:27,060
The next time unit,

40
00:02:27,060 --> 00:02:30,080
it's going to be
able to transmit one

41
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:34,180
of those packets so
it'll transmit Packet 1.

42
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:39,100
That's between this
line or this column.

43
00:02:39,100 --> 00:02:42,415
In this column it's able
to transmit Packet 1,

44
00:02:42,415 --> 00:02:46,980
but then it's going to
receive Packets 3, and 4.

45
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:51,145
Then it's going to be able
to transmit Packet 2,

46
00:02:51,145 --> 00:02:53,320
and it's going to
receive five and six.

47
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:57,660
Transmit Packet 3,

48
00:02:57,660 --> 00:03:00,735
and then receive packets
seven, and eight.

49
00:03:00,735 --> 00:03:04,125
You can see this buffer

50
00:03:04,125 --> 00:03:06,560
is just increasing the number

51
00:03:06,560 --> 00:03:08,280
of packets that are queued up,

52
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:13,420
ready to be transmitted
increases over time.

53
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:17,660
This is starting
to be congestion.

54
00:03:18,220 --> 00:03:21,760
What happens when
congestion occurs?

55
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:24,580
One of the things could be

56
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:28,620
packets get dropped
by the router.

57
00:03:28,620 --> 00:03:30,840
That's when we saw that there's

58
00:03:30,840 --> 00:03:34,080
only a limited amount of
memory in these packet cues or

59
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:36,300
the packet buffers and so

60
00:03:36,300 --> 00:03:38,740
the number of packets that we

61
00:03:38,740 --> 00:03:41,860
have to buffer up is too high.

62
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:44,420
The router will
start dropping them.

63
00:03:44,420 --> 00:03:46,805
Then what happens on the host?

64
00:03:46,805 --> 00:03:48,900
Well, we saw with TCP, well,

65
00:03:48,900 --> 00:03:51,900
it's going to detect
that something wasn't

66
00:03:51,900 --> 00:03:57,370
received and it's going to be
re-transmitted by the host.

67
00:03:57,470 --> 00:04:00,960
When it becomes
re-transmitted, what happens?

68
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:05,260
It just goes back into
that router that's already

69
00:04:05,260 --> 00:04:11,100
congested and compounds
the problem right there.

70
00:04:11,420 --> 00:04:13,920
The end result of this,

71
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:16,100
if we don't actually
take care of it,

72
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:19,600
is something called
congestion collapse.

73
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:23,180
When we're nearing
this capacity,

74
00:04:23,180 --> 00:04:26,890
the goodput decreases rapidly.

75
00:04:26,890 --> 00:04:28,120
What does goodput mean?

76
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:33,020
That's basically actual
packets that make it

77
00:04:33,020 --> 00:04:35,140
all the way from the source to

78
00:04:35,140 --> 00:04:38,995
the destination
containing unique data.

79
00:04:38,995 --> 00:04:43,800
The re-transmitted data
would decrease goodput,

80
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:47,920
anything getting dropped
decreases the goodput.

81
00:04:48,020 --> 00:04:50,880
As we're talking about

82
00:04:50,880 --> 00:04:53,540
just now is the buffers
get full but the

83
00:04:53,540 --> 00:04:55,560
sending keeps overloading them

84
00:04:55,560 --> 00:04:57,100
so more and more packets are

85
00:04:57,100 --> 00:04:59,750
getting dropped and
re-transmitted.

86
00:04:59,980 --> 00:05:03,590
What this leads to is
congestion collapse.

87
00:05:03,590 --> 00:05:05,270
When we say offered load,

88
00:05:05,270 --> 00:05:07,010
that's the amount being sent.

89
00:05:07,010 --> 00:05:11,165
As you increase the number
of packets being sent,

90
00:05:11,165 --> 00:05:13,850
the Good-put is the
number of those that are

91
00:05:13,850 --> 00:05:17,570
successfully making it
to the destination.

92
00:05:17,570 --> 00:05:21,245
At some point, it just collapse.

93
00:05:21,245 --> 00:05:22,775
You keep sending more and more,

94
00:05:22,775 --> 00:05:24,200
but then actually less and

95
00:05:24,200 --> 00:05:27,600
less makes it to
the destination.

96
00:05:29,530 --> 00:05:32,360
This is where we introduced

97
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:35,225
the concept of
congestion control.

98
00:05:35,225 --> 00:05:36,830
Can we get the network to

99
00:05:36,830 --> 00:05:39,800
operate just before
it collapses?

100
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:44,540
Our goal is to make it
efficient and fair.

101
00:05:44,540 --> 00:05:46,910
We want to be able to put in

102
00:05:46,910 --> 00:05:49,280
some control knobs
to be able to say,

103
00:05:49,280 --> 00:05:52,220
well, at this point, you're
starting to hit this point.

104
00:05:52,220 --> 00:05:54,080
Maybe we should not be

105
00:05:54,080 --> 00:05:57,200
increasing the
offered load anymore.

106
00:05:57,200 --> 00:06:01,040
But we got to do it in an
efficient and fair manner.

107
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:04,265
What does efficient
and fair mean?

108
00:06:04,265 --> 00:06:07,445
Here we can illustrate it.

109
00:06:07,445 --> 00:06:09,530
For fair means that

110
00:06:09,530 --> 00:06:13,040
each flow receives an equal
share of the bandwidth on

111
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:15,575
a link and then efficient

112
00:06:15,575 --> 00:06:18,995
means the full bandwidth
of a link is used.

113
00:06:18,995 --> 00:06:20,885
To illustrate this,

114
00:06:20,885 --> 00:06:25,230
let's say we have those
two hosts or two flows.

115
00:06:26,230 --> 00:06:28,250
This is the throughput.

116
00:06:28,250 --> 00:06:30,440
We're saying that
the link can handle

117
00:06:30,440 --> 00:06:33,800
one unit of throughput.

118
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:37,680
One packet per second
or whatever that is.

119
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:45,275
What this blue line
represents is efficient,

120
00:06:45,275 --> 00:06:48,515
where the actual
link is handling

121
00:06:48,515 --> 00:06:54,140
one unit of traffic
per unit time.

122
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:56,675
This could be all coming from

123
00:06:56,675 --> 00:06:59,270
Host 2 where it's

124
00:06:59,270 --> 00:07:02,660
sending and Host 1 is
not sending anything,

125
00:07:02,660 --> 00:07:05,540
or it could be Host 1 is

126
00:07:05,540 --> 00:07:07,400
sending one full unit and

127
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:09,650
Host 2 is not sending anything,

128
00:07:09,650 --> 00:07:11,735
or anywhere in between.

129
00:07:11,735 --> 00:07:14,720
In this line, it's efficient.

130
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:16,925
We're using the full bandwidth.

131
00:07:16,925 --> 00:07:19,415
Fair is the concept of

132
00:07:19,415 --> 00:07:21,635
where they're sending
equal amount.

133
00:07:21,635 --> 00:07:24,680
It's represented by
this green line.

134
00:07:24,680 --> 00:07:30,020
You could see whatever we
increase it to both Host 1

135
00:07:30,020 --> 00:07:35,195
and Host 2 are sending the
same amount, so that's fair.

136
00:07:35,195 --> 00:07:37,070
Efficient and fair.

137
00:07:37,070 --> 00:07:38,990
We want to get it as close to

138
00:07:38,990 --> 00:07:42,695
that point as possible
right where they intersect.

139
00:07:42,695 --> 00:07:45,905
Now, anything above
this blue line,

140
00:07:45,905 --> 00:07:49,580
all these red lines
are indicated.

141
00:07:49,580 --> 00:07:52,715
This is the point where
congestion will happen.

142
00:07:52,715 --> 00:07:55,670
If the combination
between Host 1 and

143
00:07:55,670 --> 00:07:59,585
Host 2 is above this blue line,

144
00:07:59,585 --> 00:08:02,180
that means they're collectively

145
00:08:02,180 --> 00:08:06,920
sending more than that
link can transmit.

146
00:08:06,920 --> 00:08:10,350
That's where congestion occurs.

147
00:08:11,410 --> 00:08:16,970
There's two basic general
approaches that you can take

148
00:08:16,970 --> 00:08:20,765
towards this resource
allocation problem

149
00:08:20,765 --> 00:08:22,670
to be able to handle
this congestion,

150
00:08:22,670 --> 00:08:24,260
because that's
really what this is.

151
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:26,975
We want to be able to say Host 1

152
00:08:26,975 --> 00:08:30,305
or flow 1 to send this amount,

153
00:08:30,305 --> 00:08:32,210
Host 2 or flow 2,

154
00:08:32,210 --> 00:08:33,530
you get to send this amount.

155
00:08:33,530 --> 00:08:36,290
If you do, we won't
hit congestion.

156
00:08:36,290 --> 00:08:41,255
We can do this either in
a reservation method,

157
00:08:41,255 --> 00:08:43,115
where in the network,

158
00:08:43,115 --> 00:08:47,610
you can reserve the
bandwidth on each router.

159
00:08:47,950 --> 00:08:54,185
The other approach is a
feedback and adjust approach.

160
00:08:54,185 --> 00:08:57,590
The hosts can
detect that there's

161
00:08:57,590 --> 00:09:02,340
congestion and adjust the
send rate accordingly.

162
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:07,715
When we get into TCP
congestion control,

163
00:09:07,715 --> 00:09:10,130
next time we'll dive into this.

164
00:09:10,130 --> 00:09:11,615
But as an overview,

165
00:09:11,615 --> 00:09:15,680
TCP falls more under
that second category.

166
00:09:15,680 --> 00:09:18,380
Here, the senders can increase

167
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:21,185
the sending rate until
congestion occurs,

168
00:09:21,185 --> 00:09:23,900
then it decreases the send rate.

169
00:09:23,900 --> 00:09:27,320
The feedback that it
receives is implicit,

170
00:09:27,320 --> 00:09:29,585
and we'll talk about
what those signals are

171
00:09:29,585 --> 00:09:34,685
and uses a window
based adjustment.

172
00:09:34,685 --> 00:09:39,560
It's detecting that there's

173
00:09:39,560 --> 00:09:42,635
congestion and
adjusting accordingly,

174
00:09:42,635 --> 00:09:44,940
and so that's TCP.

175
00:09:45,940 --> 00:09:49,235
Next time we'll dive into

176
00:09:49,235 --> 00:09:52,370
TCP's congestion
control mechanisms.

177
00:09:52,370 --> 00:10:01,170
Thanks.